{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789779179,"nonce":"K6E9BREXksJCNFeu7fymbQPb3d0=","title":"A loop in a function returning through a destination never leaves the interpreter","message":"The interpreter emits OSR sites only when a function does not return\nthrough a caller-provided destination, and osr_layout_with rejects any\nreturn type wider than a register (ReturnDoesntFit), so no loop in such a\nfunction is ever transferred: a 2M-iteration loop in a function returning\na shaped tuple ran interpreted end to end (650 ns per iteration against\n13 ns for PyPy), and Cranelift tier-0 code for such functions gets no\nprobes either. The list library is in the same position: zb_list_range,\nzb_list_filter, zb_set_sub and every other function returning a list\nheader shows the same rejection.\n\nFix: the OSR frame carries the destination pointer in a slot of its own,\nthe helper writes its result through it and returns it (the function's own\nconvention), and the interpreter, the Cranelift probe and the Cranelift\nand LLVM helper prologues read the slot. Then the gates go.","files":null}]}